From a2843e8e755f132f1041bcdc3b7f90c9cc912967 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Facilis <dev@facilis.local>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 18:52:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(opfs): merge the mirror's index across tabs, and replace
 it atomically
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The write-behind mirror is per ORIGIN, not per tab: a second Studio tab restores
the same tree and mirrors its own writes into the same OPFS directory. The index
was rewritten as a whole from this kernel's in-memory meta, so a tab that had
never seen another tab's paths silently dropped them — measured with two tabs
(React + Vue, same origin): after tab A wrote one file the index held 0 of tab
B's 10 paths, and reloading tab B lost its whole project (vv-stat answered that
it does not exist).

Track the paths THIS kernel changed (writes, deletes, and entries restore() now
drops because shouldPersist rejects them) and apply exactly those to the index
that is on disk, under a 'vv-vfs-manifest' Web Lock so two tabs cannot
interleave the read-modify-write.

The index also has to be replaced atomically. OPFS has no rename, and
truncate(0)+write() leaves the file EMPTY for a few milliseconds, so a tab that
booted during that window read an empty file, failed to parse it and restored
NOTHING — measured, with everything else in the index intact. Writers now stage
a complete 'manifest.json.new' and move() it over the live index, and readers
try the staged copy and retry instead of treating 'mid-write' as 'nothing
saved'. An index that exists but still cannot be parsed is never overwritten
with the kernel's own (empty) view; the error is logged once and the write is
deferred.

Verified with two real tabs (and a third one refused for holding a project the
first tab owns): after one tab writes and the other reloads, both projects are
restored, and the index keeps both tabs' paths.
